WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:05.240
Years ago, I made two videos on strange elevators
that go both horizontally and vertically.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.840
The first was in Genoa, Italy. 

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.640
It's a little cabin that
travels along a tunnel 

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.360
which then gets loaded
 into a regular vertical elevator.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.080
The second is in a test tower
in Rottweil in Germany.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.920
It uses fancy, complicated locking mechanisms
and linear induction motors.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.800
But the key thing about both those designs is:
they come to a complete stop when changing direction. 

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.720
Because of how elevators work,
safely and smoothly transitioning

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.800
from horizontal to vertical
just isn't possible.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.240
Or at least I thought so. 

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.520
But it turns out
that the Schmid Peoplemover

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:35.920
has been doing exactly that
for about 20 years.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.840
Only three of these have ever
actually been installed. 

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:38.920
Only two of them still exist. 

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.880
This is the larger one, at a train station
in Altbach, Germany.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.920
It took me a long time to understand
how this works, and how it works safely.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.240
I had to hire an engineering consultant to
look at the original patents

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:50.800
and break it down for me.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.560
And also, with the help of
a producer in Germany...

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:54.760
I tracked down the inventor.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.340
[IN GERMAN:]
 
 
 

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.040
This cabin isn't being pulled by cables,
the motor is in the cabin itself,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.240
it pulls itself along by
turning a pair of cogs up top

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.480
that sit on rack rails,
one on each side of the cabin.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.320
So this is basically a cog railway.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.880
A very fancy one with anti-shake devices,
but a suspended cog railway.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.080
And that explains the smooth transition
to vertical at the top: the cogs just

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.960
follow the rails as they curve. 

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.840
But how does it do this safely, 

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.240
and then travel vertically without,
you know, falling to the ground?

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.720
Well, like the elevator in Genoa, it does
get a bit of help on the vertical part.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.680
It locks onto a carrier device
and a counterweight, with safety systems

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.760
that are roughly equivalent
to a regular elevator. 

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.760
The carrier is what you can see
start moving through the window

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.600
as you transition to vertical movement. 

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.000
And meanwhile, down on the bottom,
the counterweight

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.760
sits on calibrated springs, so that as this
cabin started to transfer to vertical,

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.560
the counterweight gently lifted
away from the springs...

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.000
and everything balanced. 

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.640
There is an elegant but also
quite complicated

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.720
docking and undocking mechanism up there
in the roof of the cabin and the carrier.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.680
The closest analogy I can come up with
is that it's a bit like closing

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:42.000
an up-and-over garage door.
Everything just sort of slides into place.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.760
It's a wonderful design,
but it's also complicated.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.680
And the alternative is to
put in an underpass, or

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.720
two mass-produced elevators
and a footbridge between them.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.160
Because if one of those elevators
breaks down, well, it can be fixed

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.520
by any qualified technician using
off-the-shelf parts instead of someone

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.080
who has to be specially trained
for this one rare bit of equipment.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.400
And if you have a footbridge,
you probably also have stairs.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.280
So if one of those elevators breaks down,
there's a backup.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.360
It doesn't work for everyone,
but it's better than nothing.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:55.960
And that also means that if you are late
for your train and physically able,

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:00.880
you can just run up, across, and down the footbridge
instead of waiting for the Peoplemover to arrive. 

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.880
Now, that said, there are
advantages to this system.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.960
It requires much less land than
a regular footbridge does.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.920
It doesn't need tunneling
like an underpass. 

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.520
And if you have heavy or bulky bags,
well, you don't have to

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.840
haul them in and out of multiple elevators
and across the footbridge,

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.520
it just takes them across for you.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.240
I think there is a possible world
in which this design became popular,

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:56.560
and the parts became off-the-shelf,
and this became the standard.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.080
I don't think it's a bad invention. 

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.880
It's very clever.
 It solves a specific problem.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:03.600
But when there are more common
and more reliable alternatives

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.080
that are just a bit more
hassle to install...

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.680
well, not many places
wanted to take the risk.

